WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
Benchmark creation.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:06.016
Public benchmarks can't say which model is best for your task.

3
00:00:06.016 --> 00:00:07.633
Let's build a test that can.

4
00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:10.900
Let's compare the two kinds of benchmark.

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.650
Public ones like MMLU are saturated.

6
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.100
The top models cluster within noise.

7
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.933
They're also contaminated: their test sets leaked into training data long ago.

8
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:25.600
A private one uses your inputs, truth you checked, and no model has seen it.

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.467
Three ingredients make a good domain benchmark.

10
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:35.233
First, representative inputs from real user queries: easy, hard, and edge cases.

11
00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:39.156
Second, hard negatives: questions the corpus can't answer.

12
00:00:39.156 --> 00:00:40.967
Models that bluff get caught.

13
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:45.900
Third, a scoring script: exact match, or an LLM judge with a rubric.

14
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.367
Put it together and the recipe is simple.

15
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.100
Start with real inputs.

16
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.500
Attach truth that a human verified.

17
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.367
Let a script grade every answer unattended.

18
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.333
Out comes one number per model.

19
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:01.367
Here is a tiny version in code.

20
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:03.648
Each case is one line of data.

21
00:01:03.648 --> 00:01:08.933
This one is a hard negative: the expected answer is that the corpus has nothing.

22
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:13.467
The scorer asks the system, grades each answer, and prints a single score.

23
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:16.818
Here's what it looks like in practice.

24
00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:19.367
Thirty questions, six of them hard negatives.

25
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:24.100
Overall, the expensive model wins, eighty three percent to seventy one.

26
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.758
But on hard negatives it's six of six against two of six.

27
00:01:27.758 --> 00:01:29.467
Those four bluffs can't ship.

28
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:31.867
Now the full story.

29
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:37.900
Thirty questions over the Mission Control corpus: factoids, multi-hop, and hard negatives.

30
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.967
Public leaderboards had two models within a point.

31
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.596
Your questions disagreed, on the axis that matters.

32
00:01:44.596 --> 00:01:45.967
Nobody can copy that.

33
00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:47.993
Let's recap.

34
00:01:47.993 --> 00:01:51.367
Public benchmarks are saturated and contaminated.

35
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:55.933
A private benchmark is real inputs, verified truth, and a script.

36
00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:58.900
Add hard negatives to catch models that bluff.

37
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.827
And a benchmark you own is a real asset.

38
00:02:01.827 --> 00:02:03.667
Next up: regression testing.
